From 9950d8b45472ed6c7eae1d8b88dd0cabcf2c41b2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 11:42:50 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 058/113] wifi: ath: allow overriding the board-data regulatory
 domain

taimen's WLAN board data reports regdomain 0x8348 -- the country flag
plus country 840, i.e. the US -- even on EU units. ath_regd_init() then
pins the wiphy to the US and sets REGULATORY_STRICT_REG.

The damaging part is when that happens. The domain is applied before
wiphy registration, which freezes chan->orig_flags, and cfg80211 can
only ever restrict a channel further from there: a channel disabled at
registration can never be re-enabled, however correct a later country
hint is. On this device that permanently removed 2.4 GHz ch 12-13 and
the whole 5470-5725 ETSI midband (ch 100-140), so an AP on ch 112 was
invisible and could not be associated with.

Correcting the board data is not possible: the WLAN firmware
checksum-validates it, and rewriting the regdomain field made the
firmware refuse to start.

Add a regd_override module parameter that replaces the *initial*
regdomain only. Setting it to a permissive world SKU (0x67 =
WOR02_WORLD: 2.4 ch 1-13 plus ATH_5GHZ_ALL) lets the full channel
superset survive registration. It deliberately pins no country: because
a world SKU also gets REGULATORY_COUNTRY_IE_FOLLOW_POWER, the real
country is then adopted at runtime from the country IE in AP beacons
(or from the SIM MCC by userspace), which is what actually applies the
correct ETSI rules.

The override has to live in ath_regd_init() and not __ath_regd_init():
the latter is re-entered by __ath_reg_dyn_country() on every dynamic
country update, and overriding there clobbers the country that was just
learned -- observed as "regdomain ... updated by country element"
immediately followed by "overriding EEPROM regdomain ... with 0x67".

Adopting the country from a beacon needs
CONFIG_ATH_REG_DYNAMIC_USER_REG_HINTS and
CONFIG_ATH_REG_DYNAMIC_USER_CERT_TESTING; the latter is unavoidable
because dynamic_country_user_possible() refuses CTRY_UNITED_STATES,
which is exactly what this device reports.

Default is 0, which keeps the hardware value and existing behaviour.

Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 drivers/net/wireless/ath/regd.c | 38 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 38 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/regd.c b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/regd.c
index f15e7bd690b5..d7b60549a8ca 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/regd.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/regd.c
@@ -18,11 +18,21 @@
 
 #include <linux/kernel.h>
 #include <linux/export.h>
+#include <linux/moduleparam.h>
 #include <net/cfg80211.h>
 #include <net/mac80211.h>
 #include "regd.h"
 #include "regd_common.h"
 
+/* Override the regulatory domain reported by the card's EEPROM/board data.
+ * 0 (default) keeps the hardware value. See __ath_regd_init() for why this
+ * exists; 0x67 (WOR02_WORLD) is the permissive world SKU.
+ */
+static unsigned int ath_regd_override;
+module_param_named(regd_override, ath_regd_override, uint, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(regd_override,
+		 "Override the board-data regulatory domain (0 = use hardware value)");
+
 static int __ath_regd_init(struct ath_regulatory *reg);
 
 /*
@@ -764,6 +774,34 @@ ath_regd_init(struct ath_regulatory *reg,
 						 regulatory);
 	int r;
 
+	/* Some vendor board data hardcodes a single country -- taimen's WLAN
+	 * BDF reports 0x8348 (country 840 = US) even on EU units. That pins the
+	 * device to the US, and because the domain is applied *before* wiphy
+	 * registration it freezes chan->orig_flags: channels disabled there
+	 * (2.4 ch 12-13 and the whole 5470-5725 ETSI midband, ch 100-140) can
+	 * never be re-enabled afterwards, however correct a later country hint
+	 * is. The BDF cannot be corrected -- the WLAN firmware checksum-
+	 * validates it.
+	 *
+	 * regd_override replaces only the *initial* domain, with a permissive
+	 * world SKU (0x67 = WOR02_WORLD: 2.4 ch 1-13 + ATH_5GHZ_ALL), so the
+	 * full channel superset survives registration. It deliberately pins no
+	 * country: the real one is adopted at runtime from the country IE in
+	 * beacons or from the SIM MCC.
+	 *
+	 * This must happen HERE and not in __ath_regd_init(), which is also
+	 * re-entered by __ath_reg_dyn_country() on every dynamic country
+	 * update -- overriding there clobbers the country that was just
+	 * learned, which is exactly what it did on the first attempt
+	 * ("regdomain ... updated by country element" immediately followed by
+	 * "overriding EEPROM regdomain ... with 0x67").
+	 */
+	if (ath_regd_override) {
+		printk(KERN_DEBUG "ath: overriding initial regdomain 0x%0x with 0x%0x\n",
+		       reg->current_rd, ath_regd_override);
+		reg->current_rd = ath_regd_override;
+	}
+
 	r = __ath_regd_init(reg);
 	if (r)
 		return r;
